 <div class="card"><h3>What is the watermark on my free videos?</h3><p>When you only use the free starting credits, the videos you make carry a small, semi-transparent Lisan AI logo in the bottom-right corner. Sound and audio files are never marked. As soon as you buy a credit pack or subscribe, the videos you make afterwards have no logo. Videos you made earlier keep theirs; to get a clean one, make it again (for a short dub, a new merge costs one credit).</p></div>
 <div class="card"><h3>Background sound while the original speakers talk</h3><p>The voice separation leaves a faint copy of the original voice in the background, and it can also take part of a quiet background sound (a hum, a buzz, room tone) away together with the voice. Lisan AI handles this by itself, at no cost: where nobody speaks it uses the real original sound; while the original speakers talk it keeps the real background under them, a little lower so the Arabic stays clear; a stretch where a trace of the original voice would still be audible is silenced and then rebuilt; and a steady background (an engine, wind, a hum, room tone) is held at a steady level so it does not pulse with the original speaking. On the confirm step of Dub Long Video, the box "Keep the background music under the original voices" (ticked by default) controls this; untick it to always silence the background while people speak. Where music had to be silenced and cannot be rebuilt as a steady sound, an AI music model can fill the gap; this costs the credits shown at the price step for each gap that is really filled, and nothing when none can be filled. If it cannot be rebuilt, the background stays silent there and the dub goes on. It cannot be perfect: with a very quiet background a faint trace of the original voice can remain.</p></div>
 <div class="card"><h3>Separate tracks for video editors</h3><p>Before you confirm the dubbing you can tick "Also save the dubbed voices and the music and effects as separate files". When the dub is finished you can then download, besides the final file, the dubbed voices alone and the music and sound effects alone (M4A files). Each has the level it has in the final file, so together they make the final mix again and you can re-mix them in a video editor. It costs no credits, but the two files take about 3 MB per minute of video from your storage, and they are kept for the same period as the final file.</p></div>
